<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Connecticut</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Connecticut is a state in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> region of the north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, named after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut-river">Connecticut River</a>. Its lands were home to Algonquian peoples such as the Pequot, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohegan">Mohegan</a> and Niantic before English colonists founded settlements in the 1630s, and the colonies of Connecticut and New Haven united in 1665. One of the original thirteen states, it ratified the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">US Constitution</a> in 1788.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/connecticut.jpg" alt="Connecticut" width="320" height="252" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Pierre Demailly, xrmap authors, Commons users (SVG) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Connecticut.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Connecticut is bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-island-sound">Long Island Sound</a> to the south, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> to the west, Massachusetts to the north and Rhode Island to the east. It is named after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut-river">Connecticut River</a>, the longest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>, which roughly bisects the state. The river's name derives from anglicised spellings of Quinnetuket, a Mohegan-Pequot word for long tidal river. Many coastal towns lie less than 20 feet above sea level, and because the coast is on Long Island Sound, an estuary, it is relatively protected from high storm waves.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Evidence of human presence dates back 10,000 years. The peoples were semi-nomadic and shared Algonquian languages. In the early 17th century about 8,000 Pequots lived in the south-east, a number roughly halved by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> epidemic of 1633–34, and 1,500 to 2,500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohegan">Mohegans</a> lived west of the Thames. Their economies combined hunting, shellfishing, gathering and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> agriculture, and wampum from quahog and whelk shells served as currency and as a record of agreements.</p><p>In 1633 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a> established a short-lived settlement, House of Hope, in Hartford. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> colony of New Netherland initially claimed half of Connecticut, but the English founded the first major settlements at about the same time. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-hooker">Thomas Hooker</a> led followers from the Massachusetts Bay Colony to form the Connecticut Colony, and the Saybrook and New Haven Colonies, founded by other settlers from Massachusetts, had both merged into it by the mid-1660s. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fundamental-orders-of-connecticut">Fundamental Orders</a> of 1638–39 framed its first government, and King Charles II granted a charter in 1662.</p><p>Connecticut was one of the Thirteen Colonies that rejected British rule. At the Constitutional Convention of 1787 its delegates Roger Sherman and Oliver Ellsworth proposed the Connecticut Compromise, a bicameral Congress with proportional representation in the House and equal representation of the states in the Senate. In January 1788 Connecticut became the fifth state to ratify the Constitution. Hartford became the sole capital in 1875; before then New Haven and Hartford alternated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Hartford is the capital and Bridgeport the most populous city. Connecticut is the third-smallest state by area. Its nickname, the Constitution State, was adopted in 1959. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yale-university">Yale University</a> stands in New Haven, and the United States Coast Guard Academy in New London.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Connecticut” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>English settlers from Massachusetts Bay moved onto the Fresh River and Long Island from 1637, with or without Dutch leave, and the New England Confederation of 1643 took in <b>Connecticut</b> and New Haven on land the United Provinces claimed; the Dutch could neither people nor defend their claim.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-netherland">New Netherland · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>One of them, the teenage ʻŌpūkahaʻia, known as Obookiah, reached New England, studied at the Foreign Mission School in Cornwall, <b>Connecticut</b>, inspired the mission to Hawaiʻi and told the missionaries about the language before they sailed in 1819.</blockquote><a href="/language/hawaiian">Hawaiian · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>In the colonies the most influential early revival was at Northampton, Massachusetts, in 1734–1735, led by Jonathan Edwards; it spread to 25 communities in western Massachusetts and central <b>Connecticut</b> and waned from 1737.</blockquote><a href="/movement/first-great-awakening">First Great Awakening · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>William Bradford was governor of Plymouth Colony, John Endecott the first governor of the Massachusetts Bay Colony, and Thomas Hooker a co-founder of the <b>Connecticut</b> Colony.</blockquote><a href="/movement/puritans">Puritans · People</a></li><li><blockquote>…North America elected assemblies sat beside governors, appointed by the Crown or the proprietors in most colonies but chosen by the colonists themselves in a few, such as <b>Connecticut</b> and Rhode Island, and in England the Instrument of Government of 1653 had set a Lord Protector beside Parliament.</blockquote><a href="/government/presidential-democracy">Presidential democracy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The first published description comes from an account of an 1839 whaling voyage out of New London, <b>Connecticut</b>, to the Pacific Ocean, which gives it as a song &quot;performed with very good effect when there is a long line of men hauling together&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/drunken-sailor">Drunken Sailor</a></li></ul></section>
